<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">The appraisal process</span></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>The Insurance Appraisal Process in California, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Eight stages, from the first disagreement to a filed award: what happens, who does what, and where files most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-copper" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/get-in-touch/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Award</dt><dd>Any 2 of 3</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><ol class="step-summary"><li><b>Confirm the dispute is about amount</b><span>Appraisal resolves the value of a covered loss. Get the carrier’s full estimate and its coverage position in writing.</span></li><li><b>Build your own number</b><span>An independent, itemized estimate is the foundation of every appraisal. Without one, there is nothing to compare.</span></li><li><b>Send the written demand</b><span>Either side can demand appraisal in writing. Name the claim, the disputed items and your appraiser.</span></li><li><b>Name appraisers within 20 days</b><span>Under the standard California wording, each side names a competent and disinterested appraiser within 20 days of the demand.</span></li><li><b>Seat the umpire</b><span>The appraisers have 15 days to agree on an umpire. If they can’t, either side may ask a judge to appoint one.</span></li><li><b>Appraise item by item</b><span>The appraisers inspect, compare estimates and agree where they can, stating actual cash value and loss for each item.</span></li><li><b>Submit only the differences</b><span>Items the appraisers can’t agree on go to the umpire, who reviews both positions.</span></li><li><b>File the award</b><span>A written, itemized award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. Payment follows under the policy terms.</span></li></ol>
<h2>Before appraisal: is it the right tool?</h2>
<p>Appraisal answers one question: what does the covered loss cost? It does not decide whether the loss is covered, what caused it or whether the insurer acted in bad faith. If the carrier has denied the claim outright, appraisal usually isn’t the next step. If the carrier has accepted the claim and paid something, but you believe the amount is too low, appraisal may be exactly the right tool.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">The baseline in California</b><p>California Insurance Code §2071 contains the standard form fire policy. Its appraisal paragraph sets the framework most residential property policies in the state follow. Your own policy may add details, so always read its appraisal condition.</p></div>
<h2>Step 1: Confirm the dispute is about amount</h2>
<p>Ask the adjuster for the complete, itemized estimate and a written statement of what is covered. If part of the claim was denied, note which part. Appraisal can still help with the accepted portion while coverage questions are handled separately.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Build your own number</h2>
<p>Get an itemized estimate from a contractor or independent estimator, ideally in the same format as the carrier’s. A one-line bid is hard for a panel to compare. Use our <a href="/claim-check-tools/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">payoff estimator</a> to see whether the gap justifies the cost.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Send the written demand</h2>
<p>The demand should be in writing and identify the policy and claim number, the date of loss, the items or amounts in dispute, and your appraiser. Keep proof of delivery. Some owners send it themselves; others have an attorney send it, especially if deadlines are close or coverage is contested.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Name appraisers within 20 days</h2>
<p>Under the standard wording, each side has 20 days after the demand to select a competent and disinterested appraiser and notify the other. “Disinterested” means no stake in the outcome beyond a fair fee and no disqualifying relationship. Good appraisers disclose prior dealings up front.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Seat the umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on an umpire. If they can’t within 15 days, either side can ask a judge of a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. Umpires are usually experienced estimators, contractors, engineers or retired judges.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Appraise item by item</h2>
<p>The appraisers inspect the property, exchange estimates and work through the differences. Under the standard wording, they state actual cash value and loss separately for each item. Most disputes narrow substantially at this stage, because each side has to support its numbers to a knowledgeable counterpart.</p>
<h3>Informal by design</h3>
<p>California’s standard wording describes appraisal as informal unless both parties agree otherwise: no formal discovery, no formal rules of evidence and no court reporter. That makes the process faster and cheaper than litigation, but it also means the written file is everything.</p>
<h2>Step 7: Submit only the differences</h2>
<p>Items the appraisers agree on are settled. Only the items they cannot agree on go to the umpire. The umpire reviews both positions, may inspect the property, and decides those items by joining one appraiser or proposing a figure both can accept.</p>
<h2>Step 8: File the award</h2>
<p>An itemized written award signed by any two of the three panel members, once filed with the insurer, sets the amount of actual cash value and loss. The insurer then pays according to the policy, applying deductibles, limits and any prior payments. Under the standard wording each side pays its own appraiser and shares the umpire’s fee and other appraisal costs equally.</p>
<h2>How long it takes</h2>
<p>Simple residential appraisals often finish within a few months. Large commercial or total-loss appraisals take longer, especially if an umpire must be appointed by a court. Delays most often come from incomplete estimates and slow umpire selection.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Demanding appraisal before your own estimate is complete.</li>
<li>Choosing an appraiser with an undisclosed relationship to a party.</li>
<li>Letting the suit-limitation date pass while appraisal drags on. Ask an attorney about tolling and deadlines.</li>
<li>Expecting appraisal to resolve coverage disputes.</li>
</ul>
</article>
